WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.776
Integer values often control buffer sizes,
allocation sizes, indexes,

2
00:00:07.856 --> 00:00:12.746
loop bounds, pointer offsets, and copy
lengths.

3
00:00:12.826 --> 00:00:17.716
That makes integer safety part of memory
safety.

4
00:00:17.796 --> 00:00:26.264
A numeric value that looks ordinary in a
business rule may become security-relevant

5
00:00:26.344 --> 00:00:31.115
when it drives an allocation or boundary
check.

6
00:00:31.195 --> 00:00:35.847
Signed and unsigned conversions need
review.

7
00:00:35.927 --> 00:00:43.560
A negative value converted to an unsigned
type can become a very large value.

8
00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:51.512
A comparison between signed and unsigned
values may not behave the way a reader

9
00:00:51.592 --> 00:00:52.546
expects.

10
00:00:52.626 --> 00:01:01.214
Code should validate the intended range
before conversions that affect allocation,

11
00:01:01.294 --> 00:01:03.560
indexing, or copying.

12
00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:11.512
Overflow and wraparound can make a size
calculation smaller or different than

13
00:01:11.592 --> 00:01:12.665
intended.

14
00:01:12.745 --> 00:01:18.232
If code multiplies an element count by an
element size,

15
00:01:18.312 --> 00:01:22.606
or adds space for headers and terminators,

16
00:01:22.686 --> 00:01:28.768
the calculation should be checked before
the result is used.

17
00:01:28.848 --> 00:01:35.885
The correct allocation size must be
representable in the target type.

18
00:01:35.965 --> 00:01:43.957
Use appropriate types for sizes and
indexes, such as size_t where appropriate,

19
00:01:44.037 --> 00:01:48.927
but still validate conversions and
boundaries.

20
00:01:49.007 --> 00:01:53.301
Choosing a size-oriented type is helpful,

21
00:01:53.381 --> 00:01:59.583
yet it does not prove that the value came
from a trusted source,

22
00:01:59.663 --> 00:02:05.746
fits a destination, or matches the
intended business limit.

23
00:02:05.826 --> 00:02:12.386
Validate numeric input before using it in
allocation, indexing,

24
00:02:12.466 --> 00:02:17.952
pointer arithmetic, loop bounds, or
multiplication.

25
00:02:18.032 --> 00:02:25.546
Treat negative values, extremely large
values, zero, one, maximum sizes,

26
00:02:25.626 --> 00:02:30.278
and near-boundary values as important
tests.

27
00:02:30.358 --> 00:02:37.276
These cases often reveal assumptions that
normal sample data hides.

28
00:02:37.356 --> 00:02:43.438
A practical code review follows the number
from input to use.

29
00:02:43.518 --> 00:02:45.785
Where did it come from.

30
00:02:45.865 --> 00:02:48.131
What range is allowed.

31
00:02:48.211 --> 00:02:50.358
Which type stores it.

32
00:02:50.438 --> 00:02:53.181
Which conversions happen.

33
00:02:53.261 --> 00:02:56.004
Which arithmetic happens.

34
00:02:56.084 --> 00:02:59.901
What happens if the value is too large.

35
00:02:59.981 --> 00:03:06.660
That chain is what determines whether size
calculations are safe.
